From: v-lix <280294695+v-lix@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] avcodec/dca_xll: detect Auro-3D carried in DTS-HD MA

Auro-3D does not extend the bitstream. An Auro title is an ordinary 5.1 or
7.1 DTS-HD MA stream whose height layer is written into the low bits of the
24-bit PCM samples, so every field a demuxer can read says plain DTS-HD MA and
the side channel is only legible once the samples are reconstructed. That is
also why MediaInfoLib's own Auro detection, MediaArea/MediaInfoLib#2531, runs
on WAV and raw PCM only.

Reconstructed samples are what ff_dca_xll_filter_frame() already holds, a few
lines below where the DTS:X syncword becomes a profile, so the probe costs the
scan and nothing else. Getting the answer before playback took a second
change: find_stream_info() reaches this code but stops at the first decoded
frame, and one frame is 512 samples where the scan needs several thousand.
Widening has_codec_parameters() would make a stream that ends early report
missing parameters, so try_decode_frame() and the outer read loop each get one
condition instead. Only DTS-HD MA pays, and that is what reaches passthrough
users and the library scanner, neither of which decodes a frame.

A block opens with sixteen samples whose bit 0 is set, and three bit planes of
those sixteen are its header: bit 0 the sync, bit 1 a CRC16-CCITT over the
block, bit 2 its length and the low bits per sample the side channel borrows.
Sync alone is not evidence and neither is the CRC behind it - sixteen bits
each, which a long enough run of candidates satisfies by chance - so a block is
believed only once it also reads as one: CRC matched and bytecode walked to its
end. One such block is the whole test and the verdict is then latched.

The layout goes in the level, where 0008 already puts the DTS:X object count
and for the same reason: it says which variant of a codec, not which codec, so
every consumer that enumerates the DTS-HD MA family by profile goes on seeing
what it saw. Reading it walks the payload bit planes to ADOL instruction 0x1E,
which announces the channel-input configuration, after checking the reserved
bit every 16*m positions is clear. What lands in the level is the mask of
streams that configuration places - bit 3 the LFE, bits 9 to 14 the heights,
the rest the floor - so its population count is the channel count and the name
a listener uses is arithmetic on the same mask. Neither is derived here.

Measured on seven carriers: six Auro-3D demo titles and the reference carrier
from the Orua-D3 pair. All six titles report "DTS-HD MA + Auro-3D" after
find_stream_info alone with nothing decoded by the caller, all seven validate
on the first candidate reached, and every recovered layout matches the
independent reference parser - 32319 for the four 11.1 titles, 26175 for the
two 9.1 ones, and 63 for the reference carrier, which wraps a 5.1 original
with no height layer and so names no presentation.

Negative controls reject on every channel: the 5.1 original of the same
programme as that carrier (24-bit 96 kHz, bit 0 set in 47% of its samples, and
not one of its chance sync runs survives its CRC), the carrier with bit 0
randomised, the carrier with its low byte cleared, white noise, digital
silence, a ramp, and the constant -8354511 - the one input here whose sixteen
sample window satisfies both the sync and the CRC, and which the walk then
rejects. 20000 random buffers with bit 0 set throughout were swept as well, and
none is accepted. Streams truncated between 4 KB and 2 MB were checked
against an unpatched build: no new warnings, and a stream too short to finish
the scan stays "DTS-HD MA".

Like the DTS:X detection alongside it this is reverse engineering rather than
specification, and AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA_AURO3D is local to this tree. Built
and run as ffprobe on x86_64 against ffmpeg 8.1.2 with 0005 and 0008 applied
first; not built for the device.
 libavcodec/dca_xll.c  | 352 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 libavcodec/dca_xll.h  |  26 ++++
 libavcodec/defs.h     |   1 +
 libavcodec/profiles.c |   1 +
 libavformat/demux.c   |  23 ++++
 5 files changed, 403 insertions(+)

diff --git a/libavcodec/dca_xll.c b/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
--- a/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
+++ b/libavcodec/dca_xll.c
@@ -1349,6 +1349,310 @@ static int combine_residual_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, DCAXllChSet *c)
     return 0;
 }
 
+static uint16_t auro_crc16(uint16_t crc, uint8_t b)
+{
+    int i;
+
+    crc ^= b << 8;
+    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+        crc = (crc & 0x8000) ? (crc << 1) ^ 0x1021 : crc << 1;
+    return crc;
+}
+
+/* CRC16-CCITT over all three bytes of every 24-bit sample in the block. Bit 1
+ * of the first byte is masked out of the first sixteen samples: it carries part
+ * of the block header, which the encoder writes after taking the CRC. */
+static uint16_t auro_block_crc(const int32_t *s, int n)
+{
+    uint16_t crc = 0;
+    int i, first = FFMIN(n, 16);
+
+    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+        uint32_t w = s[i] & 0xFFFFFF;
+
+        crc = auro_crc16(crc, (i < first ? (w & 0xFD) : w) & 0xFF);
+        crc = auro_crc16(crc, (w >>  8) & 0xFF);
+        crc = auro_crc16(crc, (w >> 16) & 0xFF);
+    }
+    return ~crc;
+}
+
+/* The CRC the encoder stored, carried a couple of bits at a time through the
+ * low half of the block's first sixteen samples. */
+static uint16_t auro_stored_crc(const int32_t *s)
+{
+    uint32_t lo[16], v22, a, b, c, d, e, f;
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 16; i++)
+        lo[i] = s[i] & 0xFFFF;
+
+#define AURO_B4(v) ((2 * ((v) & 0xFF)) & 4)
+    v22 = (((2 * lo[0]) & 0xFFFFFFFC) | (lo[1] & 3)) >> 1;
+    a   = ((((2 * lo[2]) & 4) | (8 * (v22 & 3)) | (lo[3] & 3)) >> 1) & 0xF;
+    b   = (((2 * lo[4]) & 4) | (8 * a) | (lo[5] & 3)) >> 1;
+    c   = (AURO_B4(lo[6])  | (8 * b) | (lo[7]  & 3)) >> 1;
+    d   = (AURO_B4(lo[8])  | (8 * c) | (lo[9]  & 3)) >> 1;
+    e   = (AURO_B4(lo[10]) | (8 * d) | (lo[11] & 3)) >> 1;
+    f   =  AURO_B4(lo[12]) | (8 * e) | (lo[13] & 3);
+#undef AURO_B4
+
+    return (((2 * f) & 0xFFFFFFFC) | (lo[14] & 2) | ((lo[15] >> 1) & 1)) & 0xFFFF;
+}
+
+/* A block opens with sixteen samples whose bit 0 is set. Three bit planes of
+ * those sixteen carry the whole header: bit 0 the sync, bit 1 the stored CRC,
+ * bit 2 the length and the width. The length is spread through bit 2 of the
+ * first eight, with bit 1 carried along the way the commercial decoder carries
+ * it and masked off at the next step; the width is bit 2 of the last four, and
+ * says how many low bits of every sample in the block the side channel borrows.
+ * 0 when this is not a block head.
+ *
+ * Every one of the 256 length codes names a length, from 16 up to
+ * DCA_AURO_BLOCK_MAX, so there is nothing to range-check: what a head is worth
+ * is settled by its CRC, not by its length looking plausible. The width is
+ * bounded, because at fewer than three bits the header planes would not fit in
+ * their own samples. */
+static int auro_block_size(const int32_t *s, int *lsb_bits)
+{
+    uint32_t q[8], a, b, c, raw, width = 0;
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < 16; i++)
+        if (!(s[i] & 1))
+            return 0;
+    for (i = 0; i < 8; i++)
+        q[i] = s[i] & 0xFFFFFF;
+
+    a   = (q[0] & 4) | ((q[1] >> 1) & 3);
+    b   = ((4 * a) & 0xF8) | (q[2] & 4) | ((q[3] >> 1) & 3);
+    c   = ((4 * b) & 0x78) | (q[4] & 4) | ((q[5] >> 1) & 2) | !!(q[6] & 4);
+    raw = ((4 * q[7]) & 0x10) | (32 * c);
+
+    for (i = 12; i < 16; i++)
+        width = (width << 1) | ((s[i] >> 2) & 1);
+    *lsb_bits = width > DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MAX - DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MIN
+                    ? 0 : DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MAX - width;
+
+    return raw == 0x3D0 ? 1000 : raw + 16;
+}
+
+/* Where payload bit position pos lives: which sample, and which bit of it.
+ *
+ * The first 48 positions are the three header planes, read straight across the
+ * sixteen header samples. The rest of the header samples give up their bits 3
+ * and above, and from there the payload runs through every sample's low m bits,
+ * most significant first. One position every 16*m is reserved and is stepped
+ * over by a reader of the payload, which is what skip_reserved selects; the
+ * check for those bits being clear has to look at them, so it does not. */
+static void auro_locate(int pos, int m, int skip_reserved, int *idx, int *bit)
+{
+    int header_bits = 16 * m;
+
+    if (pos < 48) {
+        *idx = pos & 15;
+        *bit = pos >> 4;
+    } else if (pos < header_bits) {
+        int rel = pos - 48;
+
+        *idx = rel / (m - 3);
+        *bit = m - 1 - rel % (m - 3);
+    } else {
+        if (skip_reserved)
+            pos += (pos - m) / (header_bits - 1);
+        *idx = pos / m;
+        *bit = m - 1 - pos % m;
+    }
+}
+
+/* One raw position every 16*m, from 17*m-1, must read zero across the block.
+ * An independent check on the same bits the CRC covers: it costs a pass and it
+ * is what stops a payload walk from following a mis-read width into noise. */
+static int auro_reserved_clear(const int32_t *s, int n, int m)
+{
+    int pos, total = m * n;
+
+    for (pos = 17 * m - 1; pos < total; pos += 16 * m) {
+        int idx, bit;
+
+        auro_locate(pos, m, 0, &idx, &bit);
+        if (idx >= n || ((s[idx] >> bit) & 1))
+            return 0;
+    }
+    return 1;
+}
+
+typedef struct AuroBits {
+    const int32_t *s;
+    int n;
+    int m;
+    int pos;
+    int err;
+} AuroBits;
+
+static uint32_t auro_read(AuroBits *b, int bits)
+{
+    uint32_t v = 0;
+    int i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < bits; i++) {
+        int idx, bit;
+
+        auro_locate(b->pos++, b->m, 1, &idx, &bit);
+        if (idx >= b->n) {
+            b->err = 1;
+            return 0;
+        }
+        v = (v << 1) | ((b->s[idx] >> bit) & 1);
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
+/* Operand width of an ADOL opcode, -1 for one the reference does not define.
+ * Only 0x1E's operand is wanted; the rest are read to step over them, which is
+ * why 0x40's two eight-bit operands are taken as one sixteen. */
+static int auro_adol_operand_bits(int opcode)
+{
+    if (opcode == 0x00)
+        return 0;
+    if (opcode == 0x01 || opcode == 0x03 || (opcode >= 0x5A && opcode <= 0x62))
+        return 16;
+    if (opcode == 0x02 || opcode == 0x04 || opcode == 0x1E || opcode == 0x1F ||
+        opcode == 0x41 || opcode == 0x47 || (opcode >= 0x50 && opcode <= 0x58))
+        return 8;
+    if (opcode == 0x0E || opcode == 0x46 || (opcode >= 0x6E && opcode <= 0x76) ||
+        (opcode >= 0x80 && opcode <= 0x85) || (opcode >= 0x8C && opcode <= 0x90))
+        return 32;
+    if (opcode == 0x40)
+        return 16;
+    if (opcode >= 0x64 && opcode <= 0x6C)
+        return 24;
+    return -1;
+}
+
+/* The layout a channel-input configuration unfolds to, as a mask of the streams
+ * it places: bit 3 is the LFE, bits 9 to 14 are the heights, the rest are the
+ * floor. So its population count is the channel count and the two labels a host
+ * shows are arithmetic on it, with no table at the far end. 0 for a
+ * configuration the public tables do not name. */
+static int auro_config_layout(unsigned cfg)
+{
+    static const struct { uint8_t cfg; uint16_t layout; } tab[] = {
+        {   1,    55 }, {   2,    63 }, {   8,    71 }, {  11,  1587 },
+        {  12,    51 }, {  15,  1599 }, {  20, 26163 }, {  30, 26175 },
+        {  40, 30271 }, {  50, 32319 }, {  54, 26559 }, {  62, 32703 },
+        {  64,     3 }, {  66,     7 }, {  67,   119 }, {  68,   127 },
+        {  69,   439 }, {  70,   447 }, {  71, 26167 }, {  72, 30263 },
+        {  73, 32311 }, {  74, 26551 }, {  75,  1983 }, {  76, 30647 },
+        {  77, 30655 }, {  78, 32695 }, { 128,     4 }, { 129,  2052 },
+        { 130,  6148 },
+    };
+    size_t i;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < FF_ARRAY_ELEMS(tab); i++)
+        if (tab[i].cfg == cfg)
+            return tab[i].layout;
+    return 0;
+}
+
+/* Read the layout out of a block whose CRC has already matched. The ADOL
+ * bytecode sits behind a run of fixed fields whose meaning is not public; they
+ * are stepped over at the widths the reference parser uses, and instruction
+ * 0x1E is the one that announces the configuration.
+ *
+ * Three outcomes, and the caller needs all three apart: a layout, 0 for a block
+ * that holds together but names a configuration the public tables do not, and
+ * -1 for one that does not hold together at all. The last is not a block - a
+ * sixteen bit CRC matches by chance about once in every 65536 candidates, and
+ * the scan below offers it sixty-four - so it is the walk that settles it. */
+static int auro_block_layout(const int32_t *s, int n, int m)
+{
+    AuroBits b = { .s = s, .n = n, .m = m, .pos = 48 };
+    unsigned adol_blocks, streams = 0, cfg = 0;
+    int i, table_words;
+
+    if (m < DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MIN || !auro_reserved_clear(s, n, m))
+        return -1;
+
+    auro_read(&b, 8); auro_read(&b, 8); auro_read(&b, 1); auro_read(&b, 1);
+    auro_read(&b, 2); auro_read(&b, 4); auro_read(&b, 8);
+    adol_blocks = auro_read(&b, 8);
+    auro_read(&b, 8);
+    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
+        if (auro_read(&b, 8) != 0xFF)
+            streams++;
+    for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)
+        auro_read(&b, 8);
+
+    /* A downmix table follows two or three streams, and nothing follows fewer.
+     * Four is not a shape the format defines. */
+    table_words = streams < 2 ? 0 : streams == 2 ? 2 : streams == 3 ? 5 : -1;
+    if (table_words < 0)
+        return -1;
+    for (i = 0; i < table_words; i++)
+        auro_read(&b, 32);
+
+    while (adol_blocks--) {
+        int instructions = 0;
+
+        if (auro_read(&b, 8) != 1 || b.err)
+            return -1;
+        for (;;) {
+            int opcode = auro_read(&b, 8);
+            int operand_bits = auro_adol_operand_bits(opcode);
+
+            if (b.err || operand_bits < 0 ||
+                ++instructions > DCA_AURO_MAX_ADOL_INSTRUCTIONS)
+                return -1;
+            if (opcode == 0x1E)
+                cfg = auro_read(&b, 8);
+            else
+                auro_read(&b, operand_bits);
+            if (!opcode)
+                break;
+        }
+    }
+
+    return b.err ? -1 : auro_config_layout(cfg);
+}
+
+/* A block is Auro when its CRC matches and it then walks to the end of its own
+ * bytecode. Neither half is enough alone: the sync is only sixteen low bits,
+ * which any channel throws by chance, and the CRC behind it is sixteen more, so
+ * a long enough run of candidates will eventually satisfy both - 8192 samples
+ * of the constant -8354511 do. What such a window cannot do is read as a block
+ * afterwards, so the walk is what separates the two.
+ *
+ * Returns 1 when a block validates, -1 when so many candidates have failed that
+ * the answer is no and further scanning would only cost time, 0 while it may
+ * still be ahead. Every Auro carrier tested validates on the first candidate:
+ * blocks tile the stream, so the first sync found is a real block head. A
+ * channel whose low bit is set throughout offers a candidate at nearly every
+ * sample, and without the ceiling each one buys a CRC over up to
+ * DCA_AURO_BLOCK_MAX samples. */
+static int auro_scan(const int32_t *s, int n, int *layout)
+{
+    int start, tries = 0;
+
+    for (start = 0; start + 16 <= n; start++) {
+        int m, bs = auro_block_size(s + start, &m);
+
+        if (!bs || start + bs > n)
+            continue;
+        if (auro_block_crc(s + start, bs) == auro_stored_crc(s + start)) {
+            int found = auro_block_layout(s + start, bs, m);
+
+            if (found >= 0) {
+                *layout = found;
+                return 1;
+            }
+        }
+        if (++tries >= DCA_AURO_MAX_CANDIDATES)
+            return -1;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
 int ff_dca_xll_filter_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, AVFrame *frame)
 {
     AVCodecContext *avctx = s->avctx;
@@ -1491,6 +1783,51 @@ int ff_dca_xll_filter_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, AVFrame *frame)
         }
     }
 
+    /* Auro-3D hides its height layer in the low bits of an ordinary DTS-HD MA
+     * carrier, so nothing in the bitstream names it and the profile above says
+     * plain MA. The side channel is only legible here, in the reconstructed
+     * samples, which is also where the DTS:X syncword is turned into a profile
+     * a few lines up - so this costs the scan and nothing else.
+     *
+     * Only the first output channel is scanned, and only until one block
+     * validates or the budget runs out; after that the answer is latched and no
+     * further frame pays anything. Skipped unless the samples are stored
+     * unshifted, because a carrier whose low bits have been moved is not one
+     * Auro could have been written into. */
+    if (!s->auro_latched && !shift && frame->format == AV_SAMPLE_FMT_S32P &&
+        avctx->profile == AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA) {
+        const int32_t *plane = (const int32_t *)frame->extended_data[0];
+        int take = FFMIN(nsamples, DCA_AURO_SCAN_SAMPLES - s->auro_nsamples);
+        int found;
+
+        for (k = 0; k < take; k++)
+            s->auro_samples[s->auro_nsamples + k] = plane[k] >> 8;
+        s->auro_nsamples += take;
+
+        found = auro_scan(s->auro_samples, s->auro_nsamples, &s->auro_layout);
+        if (found > 0)
+            s->auro_present = s->auro_latched = 1;
+        else if (found < 0 || s->auro_nsamples >= DCA_AURO_SCAN_SAMPLES)
+            s->auro_latched = 1;
+    }
+
+    /* Re-asserted every frame: the switch above sets the profile afresh each
+     * time, and an Auro carrier is DTS-HD MA to it every time.
+     *
+     * The layout goes in the level, where 0008 already puts the DTS:X object
+     * count, and for the same reason: it says which variant of a codec, not
+     * which codec, so every list that enumerates the DTS-HD MA family by
+     * profile goes on seeing what it saw. It is the mask of streams the
+     * configuration places, passed on as the format states it - the count of
+     * them and the name a listener uses are both arithmetic on that mask, and
+     * belong to whoever asked rather than here. 0 for a block that announced no
+     * configuration, which stays at AV_LEVEL_UNKNOWN and says nothing. */
+    if (s->auro_present && avctx->profile == AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA) {
+        avctx->profile = AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA_AURO3D;
+        if (s->auro_layout)
+            avctx->level = s->auro_layout;
+    }
+
     if (!asset->one_to_one_map_ch_to_spkr) {
         if (asset->representation_type == DCA_REPR_TYPE_LtRt)
             matrix_encoding = AV_MATRIX_ENCODING_DOLBY;
@@ -1507,6 +1844,9 @@ int ff_dca_xll_filter_frame(DCAXllDecoder *s, AVFrame *frame)
 
 av_cold void ff_dca_xll_flush(DCAXllDecoder *s)
 {
+    /* The next stream need not be the one that was scanned, so the verdict goes
+     * with the rest of the state rather than outliving what it described. */
+    s->auro_latched = s->auro_present = s->auro_nsamples = s->auro_layout = 0;
     clear_pbr(s);
 }
 
diff --git a/libavcodec/dca_xll.h b/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
--- a/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
+++ b/libavcodec/dca_xll.h
@@ -37,6 +37,26 @@
 #define DCA_XLL_DMIX_SCALES_MAX         ((DCA_XLL_CHSETS_MAX - 1) * DCA_XLL_CHANNELS_MAX)
 #define DCA_XLL_DMIX_COEFFS_MAX         (DCA_XLL_DMIX_SCALES_MAX * DCA_XLL_CHANNELS_MAX)
 #define DCA_XLL_PBR_BUFFER_MAX          (240 << 10)
+
+/* Auro-Codec scan: the largest block the size code can express, how much of the
+ * first output channel to look at before giving up, and how many failed block
+ * candidates to pay for.
+ *
+ * Blocks tile the stream, so a buffer holding two of the largest always holds
+ * one whole one whatever phase it starts at; the reference detector sizes its
+ * own ring the same way. 8192 samples is ~170 ms at 48 kHz, and sixteen frames
+ * of the usual 512. Every carrier tested validates on its first candidate, so
+ * 64 is generous. */
+#define DCA_AURO_BLOCK_MAX              4096
+#define DCA_AURO_SCAN_SAMPLES           (2 * DCA_AURO_BLOCK_MAX)
+#define DCA_AURO_MAX_CANDIDATES         64
+
+/* Low bits per sample the side channel may borrow, and the ceiling on how far
+ * the bytecode announcing the layout is followed. Below three the header planes
+ * would not fit in their own samples. */
+#define DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MIN           3
+#define DCA_AURO_LSB_BITS_MAX           14
+#define DCA_AURO_MAX_ADOL_INSTRUCTIONS  64
 #define DCA_XLL_SAMPLE_BUFFERS_MAX      3
 
 typedef struct DCAXllBand {
@@ -138,6 +158,12 @@ typedef struct DCAXllDecoder {
     int    x_syncword_present;        ///< Syncword for extension data at end of frame (DTS:X) is present
     int    x_imax_syncword_present;   ///< Syncword for extension data at end of frame (DTS:X IMAX) is present
 
+    int     auro_latched;             ///< Auro-Codec scan has finished, one way or the other
+    int     auro_present;             ///< Auro-Codec side channel confirmed by a block CRC
+    int     auro_layout;              ///< Stream mask of the layout it unfolds to, 0 if unannounced
+    int     auro_nsamples;            ///< Samples held in auro_samples
+    int32_t auro_samples[DCA_AURO_SCAN_SAMPLES]; ///< First output channel, 24-bit, for the scan
+
     int     output_mask;
     int32_t *output_samples[DCA_SPEAKER_COUNT];
 } DCAXllDecoder;
diff --git a/libavcodec/defs.h b/libavcodec/defs.h
--- a/libavcodec/defs.h
+++ b/libavcodec/defs.h
@@ -92,6 +92,7 @@
 #define AV_PROFILE_DTS_EXPRESS        70
 #define AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA_X        61
 #define AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA_X_IMAX   62
+#define AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA_AURO3D   63
 
 #define AV_PROFILE_EAC3_DDP_ATMOS         30
 
diff --git a/libavcodec/profiles.c b/libavcodec/profiles.c
--- a/libavcodec/profiles.c
+++ b/libavcodec/profiles.c
@@ -45,6 +45,7 @@ const AVProfile ff_dca_profiles[] = {
     { AV_PROFILE_DTS_HD_MA,          "DTS-HD MA"              },
